  "contents": "Is this clause enforceable in a residential lease in Ontario? Cite the section.\n\nRead the clause as a term of an ordinary private residential tenancy in Ontario that the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 covers: the rental unit has no exemption under section 6.1, and it is not student housing, a care home, a co-operative or a mobile home park.\n\nClause: Any increase in rent during the tenancy will not exceed the annual rent increase guideline.\n\nEnd your answer with these two lines:\nVerdict: enforceable (or: Verdict: not enforceable)\nSection: <the number of the one section of the Residential Tenancies Act, 2006 that decides it>\n",
  "config": {
   "max_output_tokens": 16000
  }
 },
 "requested_at": "2026-11-13T20:08:54.008488Z",
 "text": "Under the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* (RTA), statutory rights and procedures cannot be contracted out of by either the landlord or the tenant. \n\nSection 126 of the RTA grants landlords the statutory right to apply to the Landlord and Tenant Board for an above-guideline rent increase (for example, for eligible capital expenditures or extraordinary increases in municipal taxes and charges). A clause in a tenancy agreement that purports to prohibit or waive rent increases above the guideline restricts this statutory right and is therefore inconsistent with the Act. \n\nUnder section 4 of the RTA, any provision in a tenancy agreement that is inconsistent with the Act is void and of no effect. Consequently, the clause cannot be enforced to prevent an otherwise lawful above-guideline increase.\n\nVerdict: not enforceable\nSection: 4",
 "finish_reason": "STOP",
